# Target C — inactive-source hull stability audit

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

Canonical Section 77 produces, in the subquadratic positive-core branch, one actual
convex inactive chamber `C_*` with

\[
\operatorname{area}(C_*)=\Omega(D^2),
\qquad
\operatorname{per}(C_*)=O(D),
\qquad
z_*={\operatorname{area}(C_*)\over a_0}+O(D),
\tag{86.1}
\]

where the `z_*` assigned points are actual contact-isolated source points and
`a_0=sqrt(3)/2`.

This module upgrades population saturation to geometric saturation by the **source
points themselves**. Let

\[
K_*:=\operatorname{conv}S_*,
\]

where `S_*` is the set of assigned isolates. Then

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}(C_*)-\operatorname{area}(K_*)=O(D),
\qquad
d_H(C_*,K_*)=O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{86.2}
\]

Moreover `K_*` contains a disk of radius `kappa D-O(sqrt(D))` for an absolute
`kappa>0` along the fixed chamber branch.

Thus the isolate-heavy branch contains a macroscopic convex hull made entirely from
contact-isolated source points. The isolates cannot realize their near-capacity count
as a thin or spatially sparse cloud inside the chamber.

The result does not turn any strict distance into a unit contact and does not produce
a diameter-decreasing motion. It is a geometric stability theorem for the remaining
inactive-chamber branch.

---

# 86. A near-capacity isolated population fills its chamber in Hausdorff distance

We prove a slightly more general finite statement and then apply it to Section 77.

Let `C` be a compact convex body with

\[
\operatorname{diam}C\le D,
\qquad
\operatorname{area}C\ge \alpha D^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{per}C\le P_0D,
\tag{86.3}
\]

for fixed constants `alpha,P_0>0`. Let `S subset C` be a unit-separated set with
`z=|S|`, and assume

\[
\boxed{
a_0z\ge\operatorname{area}C-L_0D}
\tag{86.4}
\]

for some fixed `L_0>=0`. Put

\[
K:=\operatorname{conv}S.
\tag{86.5}
\]

## 86.1 A coarse but uniform inradius bound

Let `r(C)` denote the inradius of `C`.

### Lemma 86.1

Every compact convex planar body of positive area satisfies

\[
\boxed{r(C)\ge {\operatorname{area}C\over\operatorname{per}C}.}
\tag{86.6}
\]

### Proof

For `0<=t<=r(C)`, let

\[
C_{-t}:=\{x\in C:\operatorname{dist}(x,\partial C)\ge t\}
\]

be the inner parallel body. At `t=r(C)` the inner parallel body has zero planar area;
otherwise it would contain an interior point and hence a positive-radius disk,
contradicting maximality of the inradius.

The coarea formula for the distance to `partial C` gives

\[
\operatorname{area}C
=\int_0^{r(C)}\operatorname{per}(C_{-t})\,dt.
\tag{86.7}
\]

Convex perimeter is monotone under inclusion, so

\[
\operatorname{per}(C_{-t})\le\operatorname{per}C.
\]

Hence

\[
\operatorname{area}C\le r(C)\operatorname{per}C,
\]

which proves (86.6). `square`

Under (86.3),

\[
\boxed{r(C)\ge\kappa D,
\qquad \kappa:={\alpha\over P_0}>0.}
\tag{86.8}
\]

## 86.2 A support gap cuts off quadratic cap area

For a compact convex body `M`, write `h_M(u)` for its support function in unit
direction `u`.

### Lemma 86.2

Let `K subset C`, let `r=r(C)`, and put

\[
t(u):=h_C(u)-h_K(u)\ge0.
\]

For every

\[
0\le s\le\min\{t(u),r\},
\]

one has

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}(C\setminus K)
\ge {r\over D}s^2.
}
\tag{86.9}
\]

### Proof

Choose an inball `B(o,r) subset C` and a support point `v in C` with

\[
u\cdot v=h_C(u).
\]

Let `w` be a unit vector perpendicular to `u` and set

\[
q_\pm=o\pm rw.
\]

The triangle

\[
T:=\operatorname{conv}\{v,q_+,q_-\}
\]

lies in `C`. Put

\[
s_0:=h_C(u)-u\cdot o.
\]

The inball support inequality gives `s_0>=r`, while

\[
s_0\le|v-o|\le\operatorname{diam}C\le D.
\]

The part of `T` cut off by the half-plane

\[
u\cdot x\ge h_C(u)-s
\]

is a similar triangle of height `s` and base `2rs/s_0`, hence area

\[
{rs^2\over s_0}\ge {r\over D}s^2.
\tag{86.10}
\]

Since `s<=t(u)`, every point of `K` satisfies

\[
u\cdot x\le h_K(u)\le h_C(u)-s.
\]

Thus the cap in (86.10) is contained in `C\setminus K`, proving (86.9). `square`

## 86.3 Oler converts population saturation into area saturation

Oler applied to the unit-separated set `S subset K` gives

\[
a_0z
\le\operatorname{area}K+c_0\operatorname{per}K+a_0,
\qquad c_0={\sqrt3\over4}.
\tag{86.11}
\]

Because `K subset C`, convex perimeter monotonicity gives

\[
\operatorname{per}K\le\operatorname{per}C\le P_0D.
\tag{86.12}
\]

Combine (86.4), (86.11), and (86.12):

\[
0\le\operatorname{area}C-\operatorname{area}K
\le (L_0+c_0P_0)D+a_0.
\tag{86.13}
\]

For `D>=1`, define

\[
M_0:=L_0+c_0P_0+a_0.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
0\le\operatorname{area}C-\operatorname{area}K\le M_0D.
}
\tag{86.14}
\]

## 86.4 Every support gap is `O(sqrt(D))`

Fix a unit direction `u` and let `t=t(u)`.

First, for all sufficiently large `D`, one must have

\[
t\le r(C).
\tag{86.15}
\]

Indeed, if `t>r(C)`, Lemma 86.2 with `s=r(C)` and (86.8) give

\[
\operatorname{area}(C\setminus K)
\ge {r(C)^3\over D}
\ge\kappa^3D^2,
\]

contradicting the `O(D)` upper bound (86.14).

Now use Lemma 86.2 with `s=t`. Equations (86.8) and (86.14) give

\[
\kappa t^2
\le {r(C)\over D}t^2
\le M_0D.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
t(u)\le C_0\sqrt D
\quad\text{for every }u\in S^1,}
\qquad
C_0:=\sqrt{M_0/\kappa}.
\tag{86.16}
\]

For nested compact convex bodies, Hausdorff distance is the uniform support-function
gap. Hence

\[
\boxed{
d_H(C,K)\le C_0\sqrt D.}
\tag{86.17}
\]

This proves the finite stability theorem.

## 86.5 The isolated-source hull contains a protected macroscopic disk

Let `o` be a center of an inball of `C`. For every unit direction `u`,

\[
\begin{aligned}
h_K(u)
&\ge h_C(u)-C_0\sqrt D\\
&\ge u\cdot o+r(C)-C_0\sqrt D.
\end{aligned}
\tag{86.18}
\]

The right side is the support function of the disk

\[
B\bigl(o,r(C)-C_0\sqrt D\bigr).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
B\bigl(o,\kappa D-C_0\sqrt D\bigr)
\subset K\subset C
}
\tag{86.19}
\]

for all sufficiently large `D`.

The same support estimate gives

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{diam}K
\ge\operatorname{diam}C-2C_0\sqrt D,
}
\tag{86.20}
\]

and (86.14) gives

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}K
=\operatorname{area}C-O(D).
}
\tag{86.21}
\]

Every extreme point of `K` is a member of the original set `S`; in the Target-C
application these are actual contact-isolated source points.

## 86.6 Application to the canonical macroscopic inactive chamber

Canonical `C-INACTIVE-CHAMBER` supplies, along the subquadratic positive-core branch,
absolute constants `alpha,P_0,L_0>0` and one chamber `C_*` with assigned isolate set
`S_*` satisfying

\[
\operatorname{area}(C_*)\ge\alpha D^2,
\qquad
\operatorname{per}(C_*)\le P_0D,
\qquad
a_0|S_*|\ge\operatorname{area}(C_*)-L_0D.
\tag{86.22}
\]

Apply Sections 86.1--86.5. Writing `K_*=conv(S_*)`,

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}(C_*)-\operatorname{area}(K_*)=O(D),
\qquad
d_H(C_*,K_*)=O(\sqrt D),
}
\tag{86.23}
\]

and for some absolute `kappa>0`,

\[
\boxed{
B(o_*,\kappa D-O(\sqrt D))
\subset K_*.
}
\tag{86.24}
\]

Thus the chamber branch contains a genuinely macroscopic **inactive source hull**:
its convex hull is formed entirely from actual source points having no unit contact in
the canonical representative, yet it fills the chamber to sublinear support error.

This is compatible with proof-only Section 81: the same chamber also contains
`Omega(D)` vertex-disjoint isolate pairs with distances in `(1,1+K_0/D]`. Section 86
does not assert that those near-unit pairs are hull edges or are spatially uniform.

## 86.7 Scope and remaining obstruction

Section 86 eliminates one possible escape from the inactive-chamber theorem. A
near-capacity chamber cannot contain its isolates only in a thin boundary cloud while
leaving a macroscopic empty convex region: the isolates' own convex hull contains a
linear-radius disk and is `O(sqrt(D))`-close to the chamber.

It still does not prove an exact unit contact or a strict global competitor. Strictly
perturbed triangular packings show why density and Hausdorff filling alone do not force
an exact contact.

The next exact chamber-side target is therefore a **protected inactive-hull release**:
use the fact that a macroscopic convex body made entirely of contact-isolated source
points lies at only `O(sqrt(D))` support distance from a positive-core chamber
boundary, together with the global diameter-minimality/diameter-pair-minimality
conditions, to force either

1. a source-preserving strict diameter decrease, or
2. an exact contact/response-ledger contradiction.

## 86.8 Dependency audit

- **Canonical Section 77 / `C-INACTIVE-CHAMBER`:** macroscopic chamber area, `O(D)`
  perimeter, and boundary-order population-capacity deficit in the subquadratic-core
  branch.
- **Oler:** applied only to the actual unit-separated isolate set inside its own convex
  hull.
- **Convex geometry:** inner-parallel coarea, convex perimeter monotonicity,
  support-function characterization of Hausdorff distance.
- **Proof-only Section 81:** compatible handoff only; not a dependency.
- **Contact isolates:** retained as source points; no KKT theorem is extended to them.

**Status:** independently rebuilt proved candidate, proof-only pending promotion.  
**Theorem state:** unchanged by this proof.
